<% content_for :title, "Home" %>

<%= page_header(title: "Home", subtitle: "Where your money stands") %>

<%# ANSWERS FIRST (answers-first spec §1), IN TWO BANDS (two-shapes spec §3). Home asks four
    questions and this is the order: the MONEY ROW answers "how much is in checking and how much of
    it is free"; the RUNWAY answers "what is coming before the period closes", which is the question
    the old period bar could only half ask; the trouble strip interrupts — and ONLY when something
    real needs a human (§5), which is why it is gated rather than rendered with an empty state; "This
    period" answers "what does each category's every rule have" (§3); and everything else demotes to
    one line (§6).

    ** THE MONEY AND THE RUNWAY ARE TWO ROWS NOW, NOT TWO COLUMNS (2026-09-06 layout ruling). ** They
    were a 1/3 + 2/3 pair, and a pair of columns can only be as short as its taller half: the money
    column is three figures and the runway is a ruler, so the ordinary screen carried a card of dead
    gap under the runway while the figures beside it stacked into a narrow strip. Three figures in a
    ROW is the shape they wanted all along — nothing is subtracted from anything left to right, so
    reading order is free — and the runway gets the full width a ruler is for.

    ** THE TILES ARE THIS GRID'S OWN CHILDREN AND THERE IS NO CARD AROUND THEM. ** `_money` renders
    three siblings (see its own note); the money region's hook rides here, on the row, because the
    row IS the region.

    ** TWO COLUMNS FROM THE NARROWEST WIDTH, NOT FROM `sm`. ** Free spans the row and the two facts
    it is derived from halve the line beneath it — at 375 as much as at 900. Three tiles stacked is
    most of a phone screen spent on three figures, with the runway starting below the fold, and the
    two halves are a label and a figure each: they fit 163px, which is what the narrow example
    measures. At `lg` the span is dropped and all three stand side by side.

    THREE COLUMNS AT `lg`, TWO WHEN THERE IS NO THIRD TILE: a user who banks in one place has no
    "other accounts" tile, and a `grid-cols-3` would leave the pair short an empty third of the
    screen. Below `lg` that user's row is ONE column, because half a screen of white space beside a
    lone "In checking" is the same absence drawn on a phone.

    THE ONBOARDING CARDS STAY TOP-LEVEL AND STAY LAST. They are the setup that has not finished, not
    an answer to any of the four questions, so they sit below the answers — but outside the accounts
    line, because a door behind a chevron is a door a first-time user does not find. %>
<div class="space-y-4 lg:space-y-6">
  <div class="grid gap-4 lg:gap-6 <%= @presenter.other_accounts.any? ? "grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3" : "grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2" %>"
       data-money>
    <%= render "money", presenter: @presenter %>
  </div>

  <%= render "runway", presenter: @presenter %>

  <% if @presenter.trouble? %>
    <%= render "trouble", presenter: @presenter %>
  <% end %>

  <%= render "this_period", presenter: @presenter %>

  <%# ** THE GIVE-WAY SENTENCE (§3's "below"), AND IT IS THE SECTION'S ORDER SAID IN WORDS. ** The
      blocks above are in `#give_way_order` and nothing on the screen says so — an order nobody
      explains reads as arbitrary, and this one is the answer to "who goes without when the money
      runs out". The three words are the app's own (`Budget::TYPE_RANK`, and the same three the
      Budget page's list, its ▲▼ buttons and the category form all say), and the door is the Budget
      page, which is where the order is changed. %>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-600" data-give-way>
    When the money runs short, choices give way first, then usage, and bills last — which is the
    order above.
    <%# ON ONE LINE DELIBERATELY: `rubocop-erb`'s autocorrect breaks a multi-line `link_to` in an ERB
        tag onto a raw newline mid-argument-list, which it then re-breaks on every run. %>
    <%= link_to "Change what a category asks for on the Budget page", budget_page_path, class: "text-status-info hover:underline" %>
  </p>

  <%= render "accounts_line", presenter: @presenter %>
  <% @presenter.onboarding_accounts.each do |account| %>
    <%= render "account", presenter: @presenter, account: account %>
  <% end %>
  <%= render "new_account", bank_account: @new_bank_account %>
</div>
